// Ask Claude Code one question and keep the answer. Nothing about the question appears below.
//
// The prompt arrives as a source-level input, so this one registered graph serves every caller that
// has something to ask. Launch it with mh_create_exec_context_with_variables, which initializes
// source-level inputs before producing Tasks; mh_create_exec_context refuses a SourceCode that
// declares any (562.120). That is a routing rule and never a reason to write a run's prompt in here.
//
//   ccPrompt   comes IN, the complete prompt text, used verbatim
//   ccResult   goes OUT at ExecContext level, whatever CC returned through the Function's MCP tool
//
// The metas are what bind those two names to the Function's two roles. Rename either variable and
// only the meta changes - the Function is untouched. That indirection is also what lets the same
// Function be called from a sub-process at a nesting level where every name carries a suffix.
//
// A caller that needs a particular answer SHAPE asks for it in the prompt and parses it in a later
// process. Neither this graph nor the Function it calls knows what an answer means.

source "mh-call-cc-1.0" {

    variables {
        <- ccPrompt
        -> ccResult
    }

    call := mh.asset.call-cc {
        name "Run Claude Code against ccPrompt and store what it returns"
        // timeout-sec is what the Function allows the CC process; the process timeout below is what
        // MH allows the Function. The outer one must be the LARGER of the two, or the Task is killed
        // mid-run and the Function never gets to say what went wrong.
        meta variable-for-prompt = "ccPrompt",
             variable-for-output = "ccResult",
             timeout-sec = "600"
        <- ccPrompt
        -> ccResult: ext=".txt"
        timeout 900
    }
}
